#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Candytuft (Potted)", "Iberis sempervirens", "candytuft")[
  Candytuft is a low, spreading, semi-evergreen perennial with spectacular bright white spring flowers in April–May and glossy dark green foliage through the seasons. Excellent for container displays, rock gardens, and edging. Hardy to Zone 3. In containers, management differs from in-ground plants. Note: Potted plant soil conditions differ entirely from your tested garden soil.
]

=== Watering

Potted candytuft dries out faster than in-ground. Check soil moisture daily in summer by pressing finger 2 inches into the mix. Water when the top inch feels dry, but do not allow to sit in soggy conditions — candytuft is susceptible to root rot when roots stay constantly wet. Water thoroughly until it runs from drainage holes. Reduce watering significantly after bloom (June) as the plant semi-rests through summer. Resume regular watering in fall as temperatures cool. In winter, keep the pot barely moist — do not allow the potting mix to completely desiccate.

=== Fertilization

Feed with a slow-release granular N-K fertilizer at planting/repotting in early spring. Use zero or low-phosphorus formula — your garden soil P is HIGH, but potting mixes also tend to have adequate P already. Add a liquid N-K feed every 3–4 weeks through the growing season. A chelated micronutrient supplement monthly ensures good dark green foliage color. Reduce feeding in fall.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Immediately after blooming ends (late May–June), cut the entire plant back by one-third to encourage compact new growth and prevent woodiness. This shearing is important for maintaining a tidy mound form in containers. Remove any dead or winter-damaged stems in early spring before new growth begins.

=== Wildlife Value

The abundant white spring flowers attract native bees, bumble bees, and butterflies (especially cabbage whites and sulfurs) during the often-scarce early bloom period. The timing coincides with early hummingbird arrivals in Commerce City, and hummingbirds occasionally probe dense flower clusters. Later in the season, the evergreen foliage provides very minor shelter value. Cottontail rabbits may browse at container level — elevate pots to avoid. The plant is not a significant food source for seed-eating birds.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=candytuft+root+rot+treatment")[Club root and damping off] (root rot in overly wet potting mix) is the primary concern in containers — prevent by using fast-draining mix and avoiding sitting in saucers. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=candytuft+aphids+treatment")[Aphids] may appear on new growth in spring — treat with insecticidal soap. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=candytuft+downy+mildew+treatment")[Downy mildew] can occur in cool, wet spring conditions — grey fuzzy coating under leaves; treat with copper-based fungicide. In Commerce City's dry climate, fungal diseases are uncommon. Candytuft is generally very trouble-free.

=== Other Care

WINTER CARE IN POTS: Candytuft is more cold hardy than Knock Out Roses but pots can still freeze solid and crack or damage roots. In Zone 5b winters, sink the pot in a sheltered location and mulch, bring into an unheated garage, or wrap the pot. The foliage may become slightly bronze-tinged in winter — this is normal cold-acclimation and the plant will green up in spring. Repot every 2–3 years with fresh potting mix.
